trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Physical exam shows stable vitals (afebrile, normal HR, elevated diastolic BP but likely incidental), diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without peritoneal signs — consistent with colitis rather than surgical abdomen. However, dehydration assessment is incomplete: orthostatic BP and clinical signs (mucous membranes, skin turgor, capillary refill) are critical to gauge severity and guide management. Since the patient is post-hospitalization and has frequent watery diarrhea, even mild dehydration could be clinically significant. I must complete this part of the physical exam before proceeding to labs.

[TEST] Please assess for clinical signs of dehydration:  
- Measure blood pressure and heart rate in both supine and standing positions (if patient can safely stand);  
- Examine mucous membranes for dryness;  
- Assess skin turgor over the sternum or abdomen;  
- Check capillary refill time on fingertips;  
- Note axillary moisture (dry vs. moist).

